<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hōgen rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1156">1156</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Hōgen no ran, Hogen rebellion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hōgen rebellion was a short civil war fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1156 over the Japanese imperial succession and the power of the Fujiwara regents. After the death of the cloistered emperor Toba, his sons, the reigning Emperor Go-Shirakawa and the retired Emperor Sutoku, contested the throne, and the Fujiwara, Minamoto and Taira houses split between them. In a night attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> and Minamoto no Yoshitomo set fire to Sutoku's palace and won for Go-Shirakawa, who went on to rule as cloistered emperor until 1192. Sutoku was banished and Fujiwara no Yorinaga was killed. The war made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clans major political powers and led to the Heiji Rebellion of 1159.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hogen-rebellion.jpg" alt="Hōgen rebellion" width="330" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hōgen_no_ran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
